 | [`manager.py`](manager.py) | The host side of one worker. It spawns the subprocess, wraps the command in the OS sandbox, and is the only party that ever reads that worker's JSON-lines frames. One `execute` frame goes out; what comes back may be a streamed output chunk, the final response, or a `host_call` that has to be answered with a `host_response` before the blocked Cell can go on. Interrupts and restart generations are driven from here: `interrupt()` returns an `InterruptDelivery` (falsy when nothing was delivered) naming which path was taken and why a failure failed, and on Linux the SIGINT is aimed at the worker's *main thread* via `tgkill(2)` — a process-directed signal may be handed to any thread, and an OpenBLAS pool thread that consumes it leaves a sleeping main thread uninterrupted. A streamed stdout chunk is attributed only to the cell whose id it carries; a stale chunk from a previous cell's logging handler or background thread is counted, not concatenated. The worker's stderr is drained by a thread of its own and bounded in bytes *at the read* — `os.read` on the descriptor underneath the text decoder, since the pipe is opened in text mode for the protocol frames — and it reports what it saw, kept and dropped, so the tail printed when a worker dies is not read as the whole of the failure. Before any execute frame or output fifo is created, an allowlist egress mode (`OPENAI4S_EGRESS=allowlist`) refuses the Cell with `egress_boundary_unavailable` unless this kernel's sandbox is enforced, self-tested, and `network_policy` is `blocked`; a remote kernel is refused. |
 | [`preinstall.py`](preinstall.py) | Package management for the kernel, held at arm's length: it is support tooling, and the stdlib core never takes a hard import dependency on it. Startup only ever *reports* what the scientific baseline is missing; applying that plan is a separate act a human or an explicit API asks for. `serve` used to apply it itself, on a background thread, resolving ~23 unpinned names against PyPI and installing them with `--break-system-packages` — so launching the daemon mutated the user's interpreter, two cold starts a week apart disagreed, and an offline one failed where nobody was watching. A newly installed package only reaches a fresh process, so restarting the kernel afterwards is the caller's job. An interpreter with no `pip` module at all — uv-built virtualenvs ship without one — falls back to a `uv` binary on PATH pointed at that same interpreter; only tool *selection* falls back, and a failed install under a present pip is reported as-is rather than retried. It also owns the confined probe every foreign interpreter runs under, which on a timeout ends the probe and everything it started -- its process group and any descendant that left it, as `system_profiler`'s scan helper does (a leader-only kill left that work running) -- and takes a `stop` event another thread can set to end it early without waiting on a pipe such a helper still holds, and `freeze_for`, which reads a package list from one named interpreter and returns nothing rather than falling back to this process's own — an artifact's environment provenance is attributed from that. |
 | [`protocol.py`](protocol.py) | The shared byte ceiling for one JSON-lines frame. The worker producer and the remote socket receiver import the same value, so a response admitted at one end cannot be rejected as oversized at the other. |
