+| [`provenance.py`](provenance.py) | Installs object-level lineage instrumentation inside the Python worker. Objects read through a supported reader carry the source `version_id` of the artifact they came from, and a later write reports the input versions they accumulated. It sees what it can see, not everything. The side table that holds tags for objects which cannot carry an attribute stores the object next to its id: an id is not an identity, CPython reuses a freed address immediately, and an unrelated object was inheriting the lineage of whatever used to live there. That table is bounded and evicts oldest-first, which loses a tag rather than inventing one. Known shared constants are left untagged: `None`, the bools, small ints, empty and one-byte bytes, `()`, the JSON decoder's `NaN`/`Infinity`/`-Infinity`, NumPy's `True_`/`False_`/`nan` and pandas' `NaT`/`NA` are each one object process-wide, so a tag on one made every later write of that value name a file it never read. A `str` is never tagged in place, because whether one is shared cannot be told: identifier-like literals are interned, equal constants of one Cell are one object, and a frame hands back the very object assigned into it, so `df["kind"] = "control"; df["kind"][0]` tagged that literal for the whole process. A read that yields a string hands back a `_TaggedStr` copy of its own instead, which retains lineage through `str`, `print`, single-field formatting and direct `%s` output, and pickles as the plain value. The worker's variable inspector and file-read observer unwrap it without invoking user conversion hooks. Plain-string assembly and other aliased values remain [tracked limitations](../../docs/TODO.md#provenance). matplotlib is never imported to be instrumented: a `sys.meta_path` hook wraps `Figure.savefig` the moment a Cell imports `matplotlib.figure`, so a Cell that imports, plots and saves in one go still reports its edge, and a Cell that never plots never pays for the font scan. |
 | [`r_kernel.py`](r_kernel.py) | Resolves a real `Rscript` and builds the file-descriptor-safe command that runs the R sibling through the common manager. Python is never silently substituted. |
 | [`r_worker.R`](r_worker.R) | The persistent R worker, holding the same execute/response contract as Python: captured output, interruption, the failing line and call, resource usage. A Cell's two streams are sunk to the host's fifos with `blocking = TRUE` — R's `fifo()` defaults to non-blocking and silently drops whatever will not fit the pipe buffer — so the host's reader paces the Cell and its output arrives live instead of all at the end; the response sets `sink_capture` to say the host, not the worker, is holding it. Output is still flushed between top-level expressions, for the expression that ends without filling that buffer. Inbound frames are parsed with `jsonlite`, but outbound JSON is escaped by hand, so an R install without `jsonlite` still reports a clean structured error instead of dying. It is an analysis channel: no `host` object, no mid-Cell RPC, no way to complete a task from inside a Cell. Nothing of the worker's own is a `.GlobalEnv` binding: the whole file runs inside one private environment (parented to `.GlobalEnv`, so a user's own S3 `print`/`conditionMessage` methods still dispatch) and the `quit()`/`q()` guards sit in an attached `openai4s:guards` environment, so `rm(list = ls(all.names = TRUE))` in a Cell clears the user's variables and nothing else, and the Variable Inspector lists every `.GlobalEnv` binding unfiltered. |
